<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Bar</h1><p class="st-meta">1033 CE – 1766 CE (approx.) · capital Bar-le-Duc</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lorraine county raised to a duchy, divided between French and imperial allegiance, and united with the Duchy of Lorraine under René of Anjou's heirs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-le-duc">Bar-le-Duc's</a> castle was built in the 10th century by Duke Frederick of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Upper Lorraine</a>. In 1033 the county passed to his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophie-of-bar">Sophie of Bar</a>, who around 1038 married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-mousson">Louis of Mousson</a>, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">Montbéliard</a>; their descendants ruled the county of Bar as a separate principality between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-champagne">Champagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a fertile land along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ornain">Ornain</a>. After a defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bruges-1301">Treaty of Bruges</a> in 1301 made the western part, the Barrois mouvant, a fief of the French crown, while the rest stayed imperial. Robert I became the first duke in 1354. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a> inherited Bar in 1430 from his great-uncle, Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-cardinal-of-bar">Louis of Bar</a>, and through his wife Isabella gained <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 1431.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>René's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-ii-duke-of-lorraine">René II</a> united Bar and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine</a> in 1480, and the two shared rulers thereafter. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, Bar passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a> in 1737 and was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> on his death in 1766.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-bar.jpg" alt="Duchy of Bar" width="320" height="345"><figcaption>Image: Zigeuner (original), Kaiser Torikka (translation) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_France_1477-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1033 CE – 1766 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bar-le-Duc</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sophie of Bar, Robert I of Bar, René of Anjou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Bar, Barrois</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=duchy-of-bar" aria-label="Duchy of Bar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4800 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Bar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5120 730L5229 694M5120 730L5054 728M5120 730L5143 733"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0M5143 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5120 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5120 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bar-le-Duc. <a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=duchy-of-bar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
